from birthdays to holidays to weddings  the pandemic has forced many of us to  put life's celebrations on hold  but imagine if your life's work is  planning those celebrations  that's the case for jess gordon once  named by harper's bazaar as the top  wedding and event designer in the world  our jamie wax sat down with gordon to  talk about her new challenges her new  book  and what the future may hold  for the last decade jess gordon has been  known in the high-end party industry  as the rock star event planner could you  ever have imagined  that the entire field you've given your  life to to work in  would all literally just vanish one day  no it's kind of like a tsunami and  honestly it's my  toughest spot what are you doing with  all of that  creativity and drive that you have  inside of you right now  i'm just thinking a lot i'm taking a  pause and i'm trying to visualize my  future which is  really hard to do i'd like you to meet  jess gordon  hey guys at the height of her career  gordon was a regular on a reality show  with celebrity chef  rocco de spirito now it's still in  progress  you like i can't believe you did it and  traveling around the world with a  thriving event production company  i think people think that event  producers live very shiny lives because  we sell luxury  we drink the finest champagnes we travel  all over the world a lot of these things  are comped because we're bringing  business  to those things but a lot of the times  too we're living hand to mouth  i do not have a dime i have no problem  saying that  every dime that i've made i've put back  into my company  to keep growing it i don't like being 50  and not knowing what my future is i've  worked too hard for that  that lavish lifestyle is described in  detail in her fictional account of the  industry  party on the moon how close to real life  is the book you know i think some of  those aspects are very very real  a lot of the experiences mirrors some  pretty awesome times in my life  so this is me as a very young girl out  in north carolina  doing one of my very first weddings  gordon grew up in poughkeepsie new york  at age 13 she got her first job as a  florist assistant  and by 20 her love of floral arranging  had blossomed into her own event  production firm  at what point did you realize and this  is going to be a very very  long time when i left my office on march  13th  i kind of thought i wasn't coming back  and then  certain clients like l'oreal paris  called and said we're a full go and i  was like okay yay  you know we'll produce this out of my  apartment no biggie and then when they  canceled  a month later that's when i knew that  was it  she moved out of her luxury apartment in  one of manhattan's most expensive  neighborhoods  come here and back in with her mother  and poughkeepsie  what is it like being back it's surreal  it's  hard to describe actually because  there's moments that it feels so safe  and wonderful  and then there's other moments where i  just feel completely claustrophobic  just with the general circumstances i  also haven't lived with another human  being for a very long time let alone my  mother  describe for me what happened when you  realized this was  what your lives would be my place in  this  is to support her nobody could have  predicted this  and this is very difficult and it's a  period of grieving and i think you have  to recognize that  and while she's waiting for the return  of huge celebrations  the events she's planning now are the  small everyday moments  of thankfulness that's sort of what's  going to pull us all through i think to  try and find  moments of gratitude things to be  grateful for  we have to every day above ground is a  good day  period and i don't know what's going to  happen in the future  let me just get through today let me  have my dreams maybe i do want to open  up a flower shop maybe tomorrow i don't  want to  that's okay let me vacillate i'm going  to fly like a little 